 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Oil at a five-month high. A deadly attack on a funeral. A vote against a separation process. This was a week of five stories, and the last one moved oil prices."
  },
  {
   "id": "promise",
   "text": "I will count them down from five to one. For each, you will see who said what, what is confirmed, and what is only claimed. And stay to the end, because several of these stories point to the same short window on the calendar. The order is editorial: I rank them by how far their consequences reach."
  },
  {
   "id": "ch5",
   "text": "Number five is a vote in Canada. On Monday, October nineteenth, voters in Alberta answered question ten. It did not ask whether Alberta should leave Canada. It asked whether the province should begin a legal process toward a second referendum on separation. Preliminary results from Elections Alberta, reported by Al Jazeera and the Globe and Mail, show about seventy-six percent voted no, and about twenty-four percent voted yes. The Globe and Mail put turnout at roughly sixty-two percent, and reported that outside the big cities, many districts voted to stay by more than sixty percent, with only two districts backing the separation option. Prime Minister Mark Carney welcomed the result. Premier Danielle Smith said she would use every legal and political tool to advance the interests of Albertans. And separatist leader Jeffrey Rath told reporters his push will absolutely continue. So why does this rank only fifth? Because the margin was wide. But about a quarter of voters still backed the process, and these counts are preliminary.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  },
  {
   "id": "ch4",
   "text": "Number four is a deadly attack on a funeral in Jordan. On Friday, October twenty-third, gunmen opened fire at a Greek Orthodox church in the village of Hamoud, in Jordan's Karak region, during a funeral. NPR reports seven people killed and about forty wounded. Fox News later reported nine dead. Al Jazeera counted at least seven dead and thirty-four wounded. Reports differ. As of Saturday, Al Jazeera said the attackers were unidentified, and no group had claimed responsibility. Fox News, citing authorities, reported that officials pointed to an extremist ideology linked to ISIS, while it remained unclear whether the gunmen belonged to any group. King Abdullah the Second vowed the harshest penalties for those responsible, and Jordan's state news agency said three days of national mourning were declared.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  },
  {
   "id": "ch3",
   "text": "Number three is a possible restart of peace talks on Ukraine. On Wednesday, October twenty-first, Vladimir Putin met Steve Witkoff, President Trump's envoy, in Saint Petersburg. Afterward, Kremlin adviser Yuri Ushakov said Putin agreed, in principle, to resume three-way talks with Washington and Kyiv after November tenth. He said the focus would be a security system for Russia and Ukraine after the war ends. Ushakov also said the ideas Witkoff brought from a meeting in Oslo had little substance. President Zelensky said Kyiv had heard Moscow was ready for an expert-level meeting. His message was simple: do not talk about us without us. He called a halt along the current front line the most realistic path. But the obstacle has not moved. According to the Associated Press, Russia has demanded that Ukraine give up the whole Donetsk region, including land its troops have not captured, and Ukraine rejects giving up territory as a condition. The Associated Press noted that the American push to end the war has made no significant progress since early twenty twenty-five. No date or venue is confirmed, though Ushakov said Abu Dhabi is possible. For context, the Sunday before, Russia's defense ministry said it shot down more than a thousand Ukrainian drones. Separately, the Associated Press reported a fire at an oil terminal in Saint Petersburg.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  },
  {
   "id": "ch2",
   "text": "Number two is a calendar problem. For weeks, American and Israeli officials have reportedly been drawing up options for renewed strikes on Iran. Axios reported on October seventh that the Pentagon told Central Command to prepare to resume major combat operations, with no date set. The next day, President Trump posted that the United States will not strike Iran before the midterm elections on November third, and said talks are ongoing. This week, reports citing The New York Times said options are still being drawn up, with a possible window in early November. According to those reports, no decision has been made. Israel votes on October twenty-seventh. Israeli journalist Ben Caspit reported, according to i24 News, that Washington pressed Israel to strike before the American vote, then backed away. That is one reporter's account, not a confirmed fact. Why does this rank second? Because it bears on the Hormuz story that follows. The war began on February twenty-eighth, when the United States and Israel first struck Iran. Negotiations began in June under a memorandum of understanding.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  },
  {
   "id": "ch1",
   "text": "Number one is the story that moved oil prices. On Thursday, October twenty-second, Iran's Revolutionary Guards navy said it had struck seven commercial vessels in the Gulf and the Strait of Hormuz. Four, it said, were hit during ship-to-ship oil transfers, and three by naval mines. Independent confirmation was not available. President Trump answered on Truth Social that Iran was making up fake hits, mines, and everything else, and said more oil is moving through the strait than before the war, a claim we could not verify. He accused Tehran of faking the attacks to push up prices before the midterms. What we can check is the market. According to CBS News, Brent crude rose three to four percent that day, to around one hundred twelve or one hundred thirteen dollars a barrel, which CBS says is its highest level since May. Other outlets called it a five-month high. Why is confirmation hard? The British maritime monitoring center, known as UKMTO, has said it is struggling to report every incident, and that many attacked ships do not report. So here is the summary. The price jump is real. The attacks reported alongside it are claims we could not independently confirm. And whoever is right, about a fifth of the world's oil normally passes through Hormuz, according to the US Energy Information Administration. At its narrowest, the strait is about twenty-one miles wide. When traders get nervous there, oil prices react.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  },
  {
   "id": "outlook",
   "text": "So what should you watch next? Three dates. October twenty-seventh: Israel votes. November third: Americans vote in the midterms, and President Trump has said there will be no strikes on Iran before it. And after November tenth: the earliest date Moscow named for resuming talks on Ukraine. That is the window I promised. Late October to mid-November holds the dates tied to two of this week's stories, Iran and Ukraine. Analysts quoted by Al Jazeera note that striking Iran before the midterms would be politically risky, because the war is unpopular with Americans. Al Jazeera also points out that in June twenty twenty-five, Mr. Trump said he would decide on Iran in two weeks, and the United States struck within days. Both points are on the record. In Canada, Premier Smith says she will use every legal and political tool. In Jordan, no group had claimed the attack as of Saturday.",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  },
  {
   "id": "next",
   "text": "One question sits under number one. How can a gap in the sea just twenty-one miles wide hold so much power over the price of oil?",
   "pauseBefore": 0.4
  }
 ],
